From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] texture_cache: the out-of-memory margin must come off an override too

TickFrame recomputes the collector's thresholds from the driver's budget every 30 frames and takes
the out-of-memory margin off them: critical_memory = budget - (margin + pressure_margin +
slack_margin). EDEN_NX2_TC_EXPECTED_MIB / EDEN_NX2_TC_CRITICAL_MIB then overwrite both thresholds,
which is what the port bakes so that a title collects from a fixed point rather than from near the
card's budget. The overwrite put the margin back, so a run with the override set collects as if the
driver's budget could not move.

A console run shows the cost. The thresholds printed "expected 3328 MiB, critical 4096 MiB" in all
63 reports, unchanged from 53 s to the end, while "Out of device memory: keeping N MiB more free"
walked the margin 0, 512, 1024, 1536, 2048 MiB over the last seven seconds. The collector therefore
never evicted harder as the heap emptied: 32 images were allocated over the budget, 49 ended up
unbacked in six seconds, and the session died there. The margin is the port's own answer to exactly
this, and the override was discarding it.

Take the margin off the override as well, so an override is a ceiling on the thresholds rather than a
statement that the budget is fixed. Both stay at or above minimum_memory.

What this can break: with the override set, the collector now evicts from a lower threshold once
pressure has been seen (up to 3 GiB lower at the margin's cap), so a scene whose textures are all
near the threshold can lose more of them to the collector than before. That is the trade the margin
already makes without an override. It does not add any allocation.

Checked: the patched file differs from the tree the stack plus 0411 produce only in this hunk;
applies on the core plus the 0410/0411 stack. Not run on a console.

diff --git a/src/video_core/texture_cache/texture_cache.h b/src/video_core/texture_cache/texture_cache.h
--- a/src/video_core/texture_cache/texture_cache.h
+++ b/src/video_core/texture_cache/texture_cache.h
@@ -348,6 +348,18 @@
             *target = std::strtoull(value, nullptr, 10) << 20;
         }
     }
+    // An override replaces the thresholds the budget check computed, and with them the room that
+    // check was keeping: it takes pressure_margin off them (TickFrame, "Out of device memory"), and
+    // the override put it back. A console run shows what that costs - the thresholds sat at
+    // expected 3328 / critical 4096 across all 63 reports while the margin went 0 to 2048 MiB, and
+    // 49 images ended up unbacked in the six seconds before the session died. The margin comes off
+    // the override too, so an override is a ceiling and not a promise that the budget is fixed.
+    if (pressure_margin != 0) {
+        critical_memory =
+            (std::max)(critical_memory - (std::min)(pressure_margin, critical_memory), minimum_memory);
+        expected_memory =
+            (std::max)(critical_memory - (std::min)(critical_memory, u64{768_MiB}), minimum_memory);
+    }
     if (log) {
         LOG_INFO(HW_GPU, "Texture cache thresholds: expected {} MiB, critical {} MiB, minimum {} MiB",
                  expected_memory >> 20, critical_memory >> 20, minimum_memory >> 20);
